#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM7033", unit: "U5")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U5.1 Tensor relations",[Simplify $(2m)otimes n+m otimes(3n)$ over $ZZ$.],[Move scalars to the tensor.],[Both terms are multiples of $m otimes n$.],[The expression is $2(m otimes n)+3(m otimes n)=5(m otimes n)$ by bilinearity.],[Use tensor relations, not an interpretation as ordered pairs.]),
 p(2,"E","U5.2 Cyclic tensors",[Compute $ZZ slash 8ZZ otimes_ZZ ZZ slash 12ZZ$.],[Use the gcd formula.],[The gcd is four.],[The tensor is $ZZ slash 4ZZ$, since tensoring the second module with $ZZ slash 8ZZ$ gives its quotient by eight times itself, equivalently $ZZ slash (8ZZ+12ZZ)=ZZ slash 4ZZ$.],[Explain the quotient computation.]),
 p(3,"E","U5.1 Unit tensor",[Give inverse maps proving $R otimes_R M iso M$.],[Multiply the scalar into the module element.],[Use $m|->1 otimes m$ for the inverse.],[The maps are $r otimes m|->r m$ and $m|->1 otimes m$. Their composites are identities because $1m=m$ and $1 otimes r m=r otimes m$ by scalar movement.],[Check well-definedness by bilinearity.]),
 p(4,"E","U5.2 Tensor annihilation",[Compute $QQ otimes_ZZ ZZ slash 7ZZ$.],[Use the quotient-ring tensor identity.],[Multiplication by seven is onto on $QQ$.],[The tensor is $QQ slash 7QQ=0$, since any rational $q$ is seven times $q/7$.],[Distinguish this from the Cartesian product of the two groups.]),
 p(5,"C","U5.1 Pure tensors",[Show $e_1 otimes e_1+e_2 otimes e_2$ in $K^2 otimes K^2$ is not a pure tensor.],[Compare its four basis coefficients with those of $u otimes v$.],[A pure tensor gives a matrix of rank at most one.],[Write $u=(a,b),v=(c,d)$. Its coefficient matrix is $mat(a c,a d;b c,b d)$, whose determinant is zero. The displayed tensor has coefficient matrix the identity, determinant one. The coefficients are uniquely determined by the tensor basis, so no pure tensor can equal it.],[Justify the basis-coordinate comparison.]),
 p(6,"C","U5.1 Tensor basis",[Prove $R^m otimes_R R^n$ is free of rank $m n$.],[Expand tensors in the standard bases.],[Coordinate-product bilinear maps detect each coefficient.],[The tensors $e_i otimes f_j$ span by bilinearity. For each pair $(i,j)$, the bilinear map $(u,v)|->u_i v_j$ induces a linear functional taking value one on its matching tensor and zero on the others. These functionals prove independence. Thus the displayed $m n$ tensors form a basis.],[Prove independence over a ring, not only over a field.]),
 p(7,"C","U5.2 Quotient tensors",[Compute $R slash I otimes_R R slash J$.],[Apply the quotient formula to $M=R slash J$.],[The resulting quotient kills both ideals.],[The tensor is $(R slash J) slash I(R slash J)$, canonically isomorphic to $R slash (I+J)$. The map sends $[a]otimes[b]$ to $a b+(I+J)$, and its inverse sends $r+(I+J)$ to $[1]otimes[r]$.],[State the canonical maps or justify the quotient identifications.]),
 p(8,"C","U5.2 Failure of injectivity",[Tensor $0 -> ZZ arrow.r^(times 4) ZZ -> ZZ slash 4ZZ ->0$ with $ZZ slash 2ZZ$. Describe every resulting map.],[Identify each nonzero tensor using the gcd formula.],[The first induced map is multiplication by four modulo two.],[The nonzero portion is $ZZ slash 2ZZ arrow.r^0 ZZ slash 2ZZ arrow.r^id ZZ slash 2ZZ ->0$. The first map is zero; the quotient map induces the identity on the generator tensors. The sequence remains exact at the last two terms but not injective at the first.],[Explain why a leading zero cannot be retained as an exact term.]),
 p(9,"C","U5.1 Distributivity",[Prove $(M oplus N)otimes P iso (M otimes P)oplus(N otimes P)$.],[Send a pair tensor to its two coordinate tensors.],[Reverse the map using the two inclusions.],[Define $(m,n)otimes p|->(m otimes p,n otimes p)$, which is bilinear and hence descends. In the reverse direction send $m otimes p$ to $(m,0)otimes p$ and $n otimes p$ to $(0,n)otimes p$, then add. Their composites are identities on pure tensors and hence everywhere.],[Verify that the formulas respect the universal properties.]),
 p(10,"A","U5.2 Right exactness",[For a submodule $K<=M$, identify the kernel of $M otimes N -> (M slash K)otimes N$.],[The quotient imposes relations $k otimes n=0$.],[Do not assume the map $K otimes N -> M otimes N$ is injective.],[The kernel is the image of $K otimes N$, equivalently the submodule generated by all $k otimes n$ with $k in K$. Quotienting by this submodule classifies bilinear maps on $M times N$ vanishing on $K times N$, exactly the maps descending to $(M slash K)times N$. Thus this quotient is the target tensor. The source map from $K otimes N$ may have its own kernel.],[Give the universal-property argument and distinguish image from an embedded copy.]),
 p(11,"A","U5.1 Tensor–Hom correspondence",[Prove $"Hom"_R(M otimes N,P) iso "Hom"_R(M,"Hom"_R(N,P))$ naturally.],[Curry a bilinear map.],[A map $m|->(n|->b(m,n))$ is linear exactly when $b$ is bilinear.],[A linear map from the tensor yields a bilinear $b(m,n)$, which curries to a linear map into the pointwise module of linear maps $N -> P$. Conversely evaluate such a curried map on $n$ to obtain a bilinear map, then use the tensor universal property. Currying and evaluation are inverse, and pre- or postcomposition commutes with both formulas, proving naturality.],[Check linearity in each variable and explain the module structure on Hom.]),
 p(12,
  "A",
  "U5.2 A torsion tensor vanishes",
  [Prove $(QQ slash ZZ)otimes_ZZ(QQ slash ZZ)=0$.],
  [Every element in the first factor is killed by some positive integer.],
  [The second factor is divisible by that same integer.],
  [*Finding the approach.* The first factor is torsion and the second is divisible. Choose the annihilating integer from the first factor before choosing a division witness in the second.

*Argument.* For a pure tensor $x otimes y$, choose positive $n$ with $n x=0$. Writing $y=q+ZZ$, take $z=q/n+ZZ$ so that $n z=y$. This proves the divisibility used here directly. Then $x otimes y=x otimes n z=n x otimes z=0$. Every tensor is a finite sum of pure tensors, so the whole tensor product vanishes.

For $x=a/b+ZZ$, the positive integer $b$ kills $x$. If $y=c/d+ZZ$, a valid witness for $b z=y$ is $z=c/(b d)+ZZ$. No canonical division operation on $QQ slash ZZ$ is required; existence of one witness for this tensor is enough. The balancing relation transfers the integer from the second factor to the first, where it annihilates the element. Since pure tensors generate the tensor product, vanishing on them settles every element.

*A tempting dead end.* Nonzero modules can have zero tensor product. Also division by $n$ on residue classes is not a uniquely defined operation; different witnesses can differ by n-torsion.

*Hypotheses.* The factors are modules over the integers. The first has elementwise torsion and the second is divisible; the same argument works for any pair with those properties.],
  [Explain both torsion and divisibility and conclude for sums, not only pure tensors.],
  idea: [Choose compatible annihilation and division witnesses, then transfer the scalar across the tensor sign.]),
 p(13,
  "A",
  "U5 Transfer: Relations after changing coefficients",
  [Let M be generated over $ZZ$ by u,v with relations $2u+3v=0$ and $4u+9v=0$. Compute $M otimes_ZZ QQ$, $M otimes_ZZ FF_2$, and $M otimes_ZZ FF_3$. Decide whether M is zero.],
  [Tensor the presentation and compute each resulting cokernel.],
  [The two relation vectors are the columns $(2,3)$ and $(4,9)$.],
  [*Finding the approach.* The presentation is better suited to a change of coefficients than a search for individual elements. Right exactness preserves its cokernel description.

*Argument.* Use the matrix with columns $(2,3)$ and $(4,9)$, whose determinant is six. Over $QQ$ it is invertible, so the cokernel and hence $M otimes QQ$ are zero. Over $FF_2$ the columns are $(0,1)$ and $(0,1)$, so the image is the line spanned by the second basis vector and the cokernel is one-dimensional, generated by u. Over $FF_3$ they are $(2,0)$ and $(1,0)$, so the cokernel is one-dimensional, generated by v. In particular M is not zero. An explicit nonzero map $M -> FF_2$ sends u to one and v to zero; both defining relations map to zero, so it is well defined.

*A tempting dead end.* A zero tensor with the rationals does not imply the original abelian group is zero. It can mean its elements are torsion. Nor may one retain the original matrix rank after reducing coefficients.

*Hypotheses.* Tensor is right exact, which is enough to compute the cokernel. No preservation of an injective first map is being assumed.],
  [Show the columns and ranks in all three fields, identify the generators, and exhibit a nonzero quotient of M.],
  optional: true,
  minutes: 60,
  idea: [Track relations through three coefficient systems and distinguish information lost over the rationals from information visible modulo primes.]),
)
